.\" Manpage for pairmux.
.\" Written in roff; kept concise and regenerated by hand alongside releases.
.TH PAIRMUX 1 "2026-10-14" "pairmux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pairmux \- reliable terminal primitives for AI agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pairmux
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR SOCKET ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pairmux
is an Agent\-Computer Interface (ACI) layer built on top of a terminal
multiplexer:
.BR tmux (1)
when it is installed, or pairmux's own built\-in multiplexer otherwise (see
.BR BACKENDS ).
It gives AI agents a reliable, blocking interface for driving real terminals
while humans can watch, attach, and help at any time. Each managed terminal is a
pane whose raw output is journaled; pairmux detects command completion
(via OSC 133 shell integration or a sentinel fallback), shapes the output for
models (strips ANSI, folds carriage returns, and reports truncation metadata for
bounded views), and
reports structured status. Output quiescence is used by
.B wait \-\-idle
to trigger a state refresh; silence alone is not treated as command completion.
For ordinary non\-interactive CLI commands, adding
.B \-\-json
emits a machine\-readable envelope; without it the output is human\-friendly
text.
.B help
is always plain text,
.BR attach " and " watch
are interactive human interfaces, and
.B mcp serve
reserves standard output for its JSON\-RPC stream rather than emitting a
pairmux envelope.
The tmux backend requires
.BR tmux (1)
3.2 or newer; the built\-in backend needs no external multiplexer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR new " [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-cwd\fR \fID\fR] [\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fICMD\fR]"
Create a managed terminal (a pane with journaling and, for supported
shells, completion integration) and print its name. An interactive shell reports
.IR created ;
a program launched with
.B \-\-cmd
reports its derived live state and is driven with
.BR send " and " peek ,
not
.BR run .
.TP
.BR run " \fINAME\fR \fICMD\fR.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDUR\fR] [\fB\-\-head\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-tail\fR \fIN\fR]"
Send a command and block until it finishes, a quiet interactive prompt is
detected, or it times out, returning a structured result. A timeout is not a
failure: the reply tells the agent how to keep waiting.
.TP
.BR peek " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-screen\fR | \fB\-\-tail\fR \fIN\fR]"
Show recent output and current status immediately, without waiting.
.TP
.BR wait " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-idle\fR \fIMS\fR] [\fB\-\-pattern\fR \fIRE\fR] [\fB\-\-done\fR] [\fB\-\-human\fR] [\fB\-\-notify\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDUR\fR]"
Block until a condition is met. The default idle condition waits for output
silence, refreshes liveness and completion state, and returns only when the
shell is truly idle; a quiet running command keeps waiting. A pattern examines
only output appended after this wait starts, never existing journal content.
The done condition resolves when the running command finishes, or when the next
one does if the terminal is idle, reporting its exit code; it needs a shell
terminal. Because
.B wait
takes no lock and records nothing, any number of agents may hold a done wait on
one terminal and all of them wake on the same completion.
The human condition hands off to a person: it resolves on a newly written note
(or immediately on an unseen note already waiting, returning its text in
.IR output ),
and also once the person is finished \(em the prompt is answered and the
terminal is visibly moving again, or the command completes. Those two outcomes
deliberately carry no
.IR output ,
because the span they would quote is the span the human typed into.
Explicit conditions can be combined; the first condition satisfied wins. A
timeout is an outcome rather than an error: its next steps repeat the same wait,
condition flags included, with double the deadline and never under 300 seconds
for a handoff.
.TP
.BR send " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-text\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR \fIK\fR] [\fB\-\-enter\fR]"
Send raw input to an interactive program (REPL, TUI, or confirmation prompt).
.TP
.BR log " \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fIN\fR | \fB\-\-grep\fR \fIRE\fR | \fB\-\-range\fR \fIA:B|A:end\fR]"
Without a selector, show a bounded recent journal view. Explicit
.BR \-\-cmd ,
.BR \-\-grep ,
and
.B \-\-range
selectors read the complete requested history. A range is 1\-based and
inclusive; use
.I A:end
to continue through the final shaped line.
.TP
.B ls
List managed terminals with their status, current command, and last activity.
.TP
.BR kill " \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR"
Kill one or all terminals while keeping their journals on disk.
.TP
.BR attach " [\fINAME\fR]"
Hand control to a human. Standard input and output must be an interactive
terminal. Attaching records no journal event and cannot itself satisfy
.BR "wait \-\-human" ;
the human leaves a
.B note
as the explicit hand\-back signal, and
.B send
warns the agent while a human is attached.
.IP
On the tmux backend, pairmux replaces itself with a tmux client on its session,
focused on
.IR NAME .
Detach with tmux's configured binding (default Ctrl\-b d). The command refuses
to nest inside tmux; detach to an outer shell or use another terminal, because
.B switch-client
cannot cross to pairmux's separate named socket.
.IP
On the built\-in backend, pairmux attaches this terminal directly to the pane,
on the alternate screen so the human's scrollback is preserved, with no session
list or status bar. Ctrl\-b d detaches; Ctrl\-b Ctrl\-b sends a literal Ctrl\-b;
other keys after the Ctrl\-b prefix are dropped. Without
.IR NAME ,
the only live terminal is opened; with several, the command fails with
.B E_BAD_ARGS
and lists their names. It works inside tmux but refuses to attach a pane to
itself. While attached, the pane takes the human's terminal size; with several
humans the most recent attach or resize wins, and the pane returns to 220x50
when the last one detaches. On detach pairmux prints
.RI "[detached from " NAME "] tell the agent what you did: pairmux note " NAME " \(dq...\(dq" ;
if the program exits instead, it prints the exit message.
.TP
.BR watch " [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIDUR\fR]"
Show a live dashboard of all terminals; Ctrl\-C quits.
.TP
.BR note " \fINAME\fR \fITEXT\fR.\|.\|."
Leave a message in the journal for the agent (or another human). Subsequent
.BR run " and " peek
envelopes expose unseen messages in
.IR notes ;
.B "wait \-\-human"
instead returns the message in
.IR output .
.TP
.B doctor
Probe the environment. The first check,
.IR backend ,
names the selected backend, why it was chosen, and the built\-in socket path
or the tmux version, and notes when tmux is not installed (not required).
Further checks cover state namespace writability, live shell\-integration
tiers (probed on an isolated throwaway endpoint of the selected backend), and
notification support.
.TP
.BR skill " install [\fB\-\-target\fR \fITARGET\fR|all] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]"
Install the embedded Agent Skill for Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor,
OpenCode, GitHub Copilot, Windsurf, Kiro, Amp, Cline, Roo Code, Kilo Code,
Factory Droid, or the cross\-agent
.I agents
directory. Use
.B \-t
as a short alias for
.BR \-\-target .
The
.I all
target writes only to agent configuration directories that already exist.
.TP
.BR mcp " serve"
Serve typed pairmux tools over the Model Context Protocol newline\-delimited
stdio transport. Standard output is reserved for JSON\-RPC messages.
.TP
.B version
Print the pairmux version.
.TP
.B update
From v0.6.0 onward, reinstall only the currently running, verified persistent
uv tool installation of pairmux, including one created by the Bash/WSL
installer. On Windows it always refuses with
.BR E_UPDATE ;
see
.BR WINDOWS .
Requires uv already installed; needs no tmux and never changes terminals.
No command\-specific options or positional arguments are accepted; global
.B \-\-json
is supported. See
.B UPDATE
below.
.SH STATUSES
.B peek
and each
.B ls
row report a derived terminal state:
.IR idle ,
.IR running ,
.IR awaiting-input ,
.IR dead ,
or
.IR unknown .
The last state means a live terminal cannot yet be classified confidently,
for example during fresh sentinel activity or when its journal is unreadable;
it is normally transient.
.PP
Action outcomes are distinct from terminal state. In particular,
.B run
returns
.IR done ,
.IR running ,
or
.IR awaiting-input ;
the outer
.BR log " and " ls
status is
.IR ok ;
and
.B wait
returns
.IR idle ,
.IR awaiting-input ,
.IR pattern-found ,
.IR human-done ,
.IR dead ,
or
.IR timeout .
.PP
.B update
returns
.I updated
when the version changes, or
.I refreshed
for a same\-version reinstall, not a no\-op.
.SH BACKENDS
A backend owns the live terminals between pairmux's short\-lived commands.
.TP
.B tmux
Each terminal is a tmux pane on pairmux's tmux server; requires tmux 3.2 or
newer.
.TP
.B builtin
pairmux's own small server: real PTYs through
.I /dev/ptmx
on Unix and ConPTY on Windows. The same
.B pairmux
executable runs as the server through a hidden subcommand, started on demand
by the first command that needs it and detached from the caller's session or
console. There is one server per endpoint (the
.B \-\-socket
or
.B PAIRMUX_SOCKET
name); it exits by itself after about 30 seconds with no terminals and no
connections.
.PP
.B PAIRMUX_BACKEND
selects
.BR tmux ,
.BR builtin ,
or
.B auto
(the same as unset). The automatic choice is, in order: the built\-in backend on
Windows; the built\-in backend when a built\-in server is already running for
this socket, so terminals created without tmux stay reachable after tmux is
installed, unless a tmux server is running for the socket too, which keeps tmux;
tmux when tmux 3.2 or newer is on
.BR PATH ,
so existing tmux terminals keep working unchanged; otherwise the built\-in
backend. Every terminal is created with
.B PAIRMUX_BACKEND
set to its own backend, so nested pairmux layers stay on it.
.B PAIRMUX_BACKEND=tmux
on Windows is an error. An invalid value is an
.B E_BAD_ARGS
error, while
.BR help ,
.BR version ,
.BR doctor ,
.BR skill ,
and
.B update
still work.
.PP
All terminal commands behave the same on both backends.
.B "peek \-\-screen"
on the built\-in backend renders a virtual screen with
.B "tmux capture\-pane \-p"
semantics; the built\-in backend journals output from the program's very first
byte; both default to a 220x50 terminal. The error code
.B E_TMUX
keeps its name but means that the terminal backend (tmux or the built\-in
server) failed; built\-in failures carry built\-in\-specific hints.
.PP
Built\-in endpoints use their own hashed state namespace, separate from tmux
endpoints, so equal terminal names on the two backends never collide, and each
backend lists only its own terminals, live or dead.
.SH WINDOWS
Windows 10 version 1809 or newer, on x86\-64 or ARM64, runs pairmux natively
with the built\-in backend; WSL remains possible, where pairmux is an ordinary
Linux program. A new terminal starts PowerShell 7
.RI ( pwsh.exe ),
else Windows PowerShell
.RI ( powershell.exe ),
else
.B %COMSPEC%
(normally
.IR cmd.exe ).
.B SHELL
is honored only if it names an existing file;
.B PAIRMUX_SHELL
overrides the choice.
.PP
PowerShell terminals use
.I hooks
mode: after the user's profile loads, an inline prompt wrapper, passed with
.B "\-NoExit \-EncodedCommand"
and therefore not subject to the script execution policy, emits OSC 133 A and D
marks with the exit status.
.B doctor
reports it as
.I hooks-no-C
because it emits no C mark.
.I cmd.exe
uses
.I sentinel
mode
.RB ( "cmd /v:on /k" ,
exit status from
.BR ERRORLEVEL ),
and Git Bash receives the bash hooks shim.
.B "new \-\-cmd"
runs its command through the shell: PowerShell
.BR \-EncodedCommand ,
.BR "cmd /s /c" ,
or
.BR "bash \-lc" .
.PP
Windows has no tty line discipline to inspect, so secret\-prompt
classification uses prompt wording only;
.B PAIRMUX_SECRET_PROMPT_RE
can extend it.
.B kill
ends the terminal's whole process tree through a job object.
.B "wait \-\-notify"
shows a best\-effort notification through Windows PowerShell.
.B update
refuses with
.BR E_UPDATE :
uv installs Windows tools as copied executables rather than the symlinked
layout the updater verifies, and Windows cannot replace a running executable.
Close pairmux and run
.B "uv tool install \-\-upgrade pairmux"
(or
.B "pip install \-U pairmux"
in the environment that has it).
.SH UPDATE
Available from v0.6.0. v0.5.3 and older have no update command; first use
.B "uv tool install \-\-upgrade pairmux"
with normal uv configuration to obtain v0.6.0 or newer when available.
.PP
The running executable, a regular non\-symlink
.I uv\-receipt.toml,
and uv's tool listing must agree on one persistent pairmux tool with a single
entrypoint symlink resolving to that executable. Verified custom tool and bin
directories are retained. Missing, malformed, oversized, ambiguous, escaped or
multiline path, and control\-character receipt/listing data fails closed.
The receipt and entrypoint are checked again before reinstalling; this is not a
guarantee against all races or a network\-free/write\-free preflight.
uv's listing may acquire its tool\-root lock.
.PP
The updater clears all inherited
.B UV_*
settings, ignores uv configuration, and reinstalls the latest compatible stable
wheel from
.IR https://pypi.org/simple ,
with persistent cache disabled and no source builds. Old exact pins,
constraints, indexes, and extras are replaced. Ordinary
.B "uv tool install pairmux"
uses normal environment/configuration instead. Managed Python, if needed,
may be downloaded separately from Astral upstream, not PyPI.
.PP
The selected stable version cannot be below the running version's numeric core.
Recognized alpha/beta/rc versions can advance to that core's final or a later
stable release; dev/snapshot and unrecognized versions are refused. After uv
succeeds, the installed executable's version is verified. The envelope's
.I output
contains old/new versions, source index, and absolute installed entrypoint,
without new schema fields.
.PP
Homebrew, RPM, pipx/pip, manual/development installs, and temporary uvx runs are
not changed. There is no force, automatic\-update, sudo, or uv bootstrap/update
mode, and no shell profiles or tmux terminals are changed. Automatic rollback
is not guaranteed. Follow the error's recovery hint: use the original manager,
or manually recover a uv installation with
.BR "uv tool install \-\-upgrade pairmux"
(normal configuration applies).
.SH UPDATE ERRORS AND EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful update or same\-version refresh.
.TP
.B 1
.B E_UPDATE
(v0.6.0+): ownership, supported version, installed uv, subprocess, or
post\-update verification failed. JSON replies use
.IR ok:false ,
.IR status:error ,
and
.IR error.code:E_UPDATE ,
with an actionable hint also in
.IR next .
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments;
.B E_BAD_ARGS
in the ordinary error envelope. uv's output is captured so
.B \-\-json
still produces a single envelope.
.SH COMPLETION DETECTION
Terminals store one of two modes.
.I hooks
uses OSC 133 marks with exit status: pairmux injects hooks into bash and zsh,
while fish 4 and newer emit compatible marks natively. If a nominal hooks shell
emits no ready mark, terminal creation records
.I sentinel
instead. For an interactive sentinel shell,
.B run
appends a nonprinting marker containing the previous status; POSIX\-like shells
use
.B $?
and fish uses
.BR $status .
.PP
A program terminal created with
.B "new \-\-cmd"
also stores
.I sentinel
to preserve the two\-value envelope schema, but it does not accept
.B run
and pairmux does not append per\-command completion markers to it. Its state is
.I running
while the pane exists and
.I dead
after the program exits; recognized quiet prompts can refine an observation to
.IR awaiting-input .
.PP
.B doctor
may describe bash 3.2 and PowerShell as
.IR hooks-no-C .
That is a diagnostic tier for hooks without an OSC 133 C mark, not a third
stored terminal mode.
.SH OUTPUT AND HISTORY
.B run
shapes one command using its requested head and tail line counts.
.B peek
reads at most the last 64 KiB of raw journal before applying its line limit.
An unqualified
.B log
reads at most the last 4 MiB before returning the last 500 shaped lines.
When a bounded view skips a raw prefix, the
.I truncated
object reports
.I omitted_bytes
and supplies an executable recovery such as
.BR "pairmux log build \-\-range 1:end" .
.I omitted_lines
reports lines removed by line shaping within the bytes that were read.
.PP
Explicit
.BR "log \-\-cmd" ,
.BR "log \-\-grep" ,
and
.B "log \-\-range"
requests are complete, unbounded reads of their selected history and may
therefore produce large replies.
.SH CONCURRENCY
.BR peek ,
.BR log ,
.BR ls ,
and
.B wait
are read\-only: they take no writer lock and record no events.
.B run
holds a per\-terminal writer lock and returns
.B E_BUSY
instead of queueing behind another writer.
.B send
bypasses that lock so it can answer the interactive program on which a blocked
.B run
is waiting. Terminal creation uses a separate per\-name reservation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_STATE_DIR
Override the state root. Defaults to
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/pairmux
or
.IR ~/.local/state/pairmux .
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_SOCKET
Use a specific endpoint: the tmux socket or the built\-in server's socket name
(equivalent to the
.B \-\-socket
flag). Socket labels are validated before the backend runs.
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_BACKEND
Select the backend:
.BR tmux ,
.BR builtin ,
or
.B auto
(the default). See
.BR BACKENDS .
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_TMPDIR
Root of the built\-in server's runtime directory. On Unix the directory is
.IR $PAIRMUX_TMPDIR ,
else
.IR $TMUX_TMPDIR ,
else
.IR /tmp ,
followed by
.IR pairmux\-<uid>/ ;
it is created with mode 0700, and the server and every client refuse it if
another uid owns it or group or others can access it. On Windows it is
.IR %PAIRMUX_TMPDIR% ,
else
.IR %LOCALAPPDATA%\(rspairmux ,
followed by
.IR run\(rs ,
created with an access list admitting only the user and SYSTEM and refused if
another account owns it. A server that exits cleanly removes its socket, lock
and log.
Socket paths are limited to about 100 bytes: an overly long socket name gets a
digest name, and a directory that is itself too deep is an error asking for a
short writable
.BR PAIRMUX_TMPDIR .
.TP
.B PAIRMUX_SHELL
Override the interactive shell for new terminals on every platform, for
example a Git Bash
.I bash.exe
on Windows.
.TP
.B TMUX_TMPDIR
Select the tmux socket root. Its canonical path, the uid, and the socket label
together form the endpoint identity used for state namespacing. On Unix it is
also the built\-in runtime root when
.B PAIRMUX_TMPDIR
is unset.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base directory for state when
.B PAIRMUX_STATE_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.B SHELL
Selects the interactive shell for new terminals unless
.B PAIRMUX_SHELL
is set. Hooks are injected for bash, zsh, and PowerShell; fish 4 and newer
provide native integration. On Windows it is honored only if it names an
existing file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <state-root>/.sockets/<sha256(endpoint)>/<terminal>/raw.log
Raw journal of a terminal's output bytes (mode 0600). For a tmux endpoint the
hash covers the canonical tmux socket root, uid, and socket label; built\-in
endpoints hash a separate identity.
.TP
.I <state-root>/.sockets/<sha256(endpoint)>/<terminal>/index.jsonl
Structured index of creation, command boundaries, aborted dispatches, and
notes. Native tmux attachment is deliberately not an event.
.TP
.I <state-root>/.sockets/<sha256(endpoint)>/<terminal>/meta.json
Terminal name, pane id, shell, completion mode, socket label, and creation time.
.TP
.I <runtime-dir>/<socket>.sock
The built\-in server's socket; see
.BR PAIRMUX_TMPDIR .
.TP
.I <runtime-dir>/<socket>.lock
The built\-in server's single\-instance lock.
.TP
.I <runtime-dir>/<socket>.log
The built\-in server's diagnostic log; read it when the server fails to start.
.TP
.I <runtime-dir>/pairmux\-muxd\-<hash>.exe
Windows only: the private copy of the executable the built\-in server runs
from, so an upgrade can replace the installed
.I pairmux.exe
while terminals are open.
.PP
For the conventional default tmux endpoint only, legacy
.I <state-root>/<terminal>/
journals remain readable in place. They are never moved implicitly, and legacy
custom\-socket metadata is not claimed because it cannot be attributed to a
complete endpoint identity.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
